   materially conflict with your task, note it in your turn result and continue.
4. Never modify git state outside the worktree provided. Do not run `git worktree remove`, `git checkout` to a different branch, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`, or `git restore .`.
5. Always read existing files before editing them.
6. Complete the full work-type contract before exiting. Commit, push, and open a PR for development work. Record your turn outcome by writing `.agent/turn-result.json` (`{"schemaVersion": 1, "verdict": "passed|failed|blocked", ...}`) before you finish, and end your final message with the matching `WORK_RESULT:passed`/`WORK_RESULT:failed` marker as a fallback. The manifest file is authoritative; the marker is the backstop if the file could not be written.
7. If the work is not actionable as specified (ambiguous or contradictory spec, missing acceptance criteria, unmet precondition), do NOT force a failed attempt. Post a Linear comment describing the obstacle, set `"verdict": "blocked"` with a `"blockedReason"` in your turn-result manifest, and end your final message with `WORK_RESULT:blocked` plus `AGENT_BLOCKED: <reason>` on its own line — a reasoned decline the orchestrator routes for clarification rather than retrying.
8. Session end is the end of your compute. Do NOT wait for remote CI, and never schedule in-process wake-ups (timers, background polls, watch loops) expecting them to outlive your final message — they die with the session. The orchestration layer owns any wait on external systems and resumes the chain when they complete.

